# 01-20-26

I've fallen off a bit keeping this up to date. Last week, I was able to get the timing belt on, even after removing it to put the cam oil seals in, and the timing belt cover on and ready to go. The oil pan is in and the manifold is on the motor too! The motor portion is all set and ready to go.

Now, I've been fighting with getting the torque converter back into the transmission. I've come to realize the c-clip on the input shaft is not seated and blocking the final portion the torque converter needs to seat correctly. 

I was looking to remove the front exhaust section to lift the transmission to provide more clearance to get the torque converter. I started to remove the two bolts, which had 25 years of road on them. You can probably guess what happened next. They broke off... To cap off that fun time, the bolts behind the cat sheared off as well. 

At least the exhaust out of the way now! But, this was when I discovered the c-clip dilemma. 

Some solutions are to craft a pipe that can catch onto the input shaft and pull it out, very carefully. I'm still researching a little bit, but there's not much information out there surrounding solutions. There's definitely tons of hobby mechanics who have encountered this same problem. 

Anyway, I'm trying to remain positive but it is quite draining, especially when it's under 10° outside.
